===Etymology 2===
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|fraughten}}, {{m|enm|fraghten}}, {{m|enm|freghten|t=to hire (a ship, etc.) for transporting goods; to load (a ship, etc.) with cargo or passengers; to store, stow away; (''figurative'') to provide an ample supply of (goods, income, etc.)}},<ref name="MED">{{R:MED Online|entry=fraughten|pos=v|id=MED17591}}</ref> from {{der|en|dum|vrachten}}, {{m|dum|vrechten}}, from {{m|dum|vracht}}, {{m|dum|vrecht|pos=noun}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]]) + {{m|dum|-en|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|infinitive}}s of verbs}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=6999029489|date=July 2023}}</ref>

The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|fraught}}, {{m|enm|fraght|t=burdened, loaded}}, the {{glossary|past}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|enm|fraughten}}, {{m|enm|fraghten|pos=verb}} (see above).<ref name="MED"/><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=1181806093|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb|||fraught|past2=fraughted}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|also|figurative}}
## {{lb|en|nautical|obsolete}} To [[load#Verb|load]] (a [[boat#Noun|boat]], [[ship#Noun|ship]], or other [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]) with [[cargo]].
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Jew of Malta|I|text=The ships are safe thou say'st, and richly '''fraught'''?}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|archaic|or|obsolete}} ''Followed by'' '''[[with#Preposition|with]]''': to [[furnish#Verb|furnish]] or [[provide]] (something).
##: {{synonyms|en|equip|supply}}
## {{lb|en|Scotland|nautical|obsolete}} To [[hire#Verb|hire]] (a vessel) to [[transport#Verb|transport]] cargo or [[passenger]]s.
## {{lb|en|Scotland|nautical|obsolete}} To [[transport#Verb|transport]] (cargo or passengers) in a vessel; to [[freight#Verb|freight]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[form#Verb|form]] the cargo or passengers of a vessel.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|text=Had I been any god of power, I would / Have sunk the sea within the earth, or e'er / It should the good ship so have swallow'd and / The '''fraughting''' souls within her.|footer=An adjective use.}}

