====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} Of a [[boat#Noun|boat]], [[ship#Noun|ship]], or other [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]: [[laden#Adjective|laden]] with [[cargo]].
#: {{antonyms|en|unfraught}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|viii|passage=a vessel of our country richly '''fraught'''}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}}
#: {{antonyms|en|unfraught}}
## ''Followed by'' '''[[with#Preposition|with]]''': [[carry#Verb|carrying]], or [[charged#Adjective|charged]] or [[load up|loaded up]] with (usually something [[negative#Adjective|negative]]); [[accompany|accompanied]] by; [[entail#Verb|entailing]].
##* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|passage=a discourse '''fraught''' with all the commending excellences of speech}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1865|author=w:Isaac Taylor|title=Epidemic Whims
|passage=enterprises '''fraught''' with world-wide benefits}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long|location=London|page=22|passage=The simplest action was '''fraught''' with danger, and could only be accomplished with the aid of talismans and counter-spells[.]}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=w:Plato|title=Sophist|translator=Lesley Brown|page=236d
|passage={{...}} all these matters are '''fraught''' with paradox, just as they always have been}}
## {{lb|en|specifically}} Carrying or loaded with [[anxiety]], [[fear#Noun|fear]], or [[stress#Noun|stress]], for example, due to [[complexity]] or [[difficulty]]; [[distressed#Adjective|distressed]]; also, [[cause#Verb|causing]] [[distress#Noun|distress]]; [[distressing#Adjective|distressing]].
##: {{collocation|en|a '''fraught''' relationship&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; a '''fraught''' process}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Oliver Brown|title=Oscar Pistorius jailed for five years – sport afforded no protection against his tragic fallibilities: Bladerunner's punishment for killing Reeva Steenkamp is but a frippery when set against the burden that her bereft parents, June and Barry, must carry [print version: No room for sentimentality in this tragedy, 13 September 2014, page&nbsp;S22]|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/oscar-pistorius/11089621/Oscar-Pistorius-jailed-for-five-years-sport-afforded-no-protection-against-his-tragic-fallibilities.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Sport)|date=21 October 2014|passage=But ever since the concept of "hamartia" recurred through Aristotle's ''Poetics'', in an attempt to describe man's ingrained iniquity, our impulse has been to identify a telling defect in those brought suddenly and dramatically low. With [[w:Oscar Pistorius|[Oscar] Pistorius]], that task is '''fraught'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 December 14|author=Robin Leleux|title=A royal occasion as heritage projects honoured: Sudbury Hill|journal=RAIL|issue=972|page=57|text=Installing lift shafts in station buildings which were not originally designed to accommodate them can be a '''fraught''' exercise, but a necessary one if the legitimate aspiration of the travelling public for step-free access is to be achieved. At Sudbury Hill, on London Underground's Piccadilly Line extension out to the north-western suburbs, Transport for London has achieved this with aplomb.}}
## ''Followed by'' '''with''': [[furnished#Adjective|furnished]], [[provided#Adjective|provided]].
##: {{synonyms|en|equipped|supplied}}

